 ## Background
-The core methodology originates from "Deep Image Analogy," which uses deep features for image-to-image translation [S1, unofficial]. The application to medical volume rendering was proposed to address the lack of ground-truth colorization and the difficulty of manual transfer function tuning [S1, unofficial]. The reference database often utilizes visible human project data, such as 696 cryosection images for thorax/abdomen [S1, unofficial].
+The method originates from the application of Deep Image Analogy to medical volume visualization [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to address the lack of ground-truth color in monochromatic medical imaging (CT/MRI) and the labor-intensive nature of manual transfer function design. The approach leverages pre-trained CNNs (VGG-19) for feature extraction and PatchMatch for dense correspondence [S1, unofficial]. Related concepts in artistic rendering include the use of VGG feature-based content loss and simple color transfer to match style images [S2].
 
